  "type": "multipleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "The correct statement(s) related to oxoacids of phosphorous is(are)",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Lqimd",
      "text": "Upon heating, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> undergoes disproportionation reaction to produce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "rm5dy",
      "text": "While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> can act as reducing agent, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> cannot.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "bZK1x",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is a monobasic acid.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uiO34",
      "text": "The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> atom of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> bond in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is not ionizable in water.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#8710;</mo></mover></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>PH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;(Disproportionation)</p><p>Oxidation state of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math>&nbsp;in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;which is maximum oxidation state of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math>&nbsp;so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;cannot act as a reducing agent whereas&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;can act as a reducing agent because the oxidation state is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math> and it can increase upto <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>:&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/a13b1161-4266-4378-b6c1-9d9e7b14bf39-image.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 172px;\" /></p><p>It is dibasic acid.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo></math>&nbsp;Only&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>- atom attached with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math>&nbsp;is ionizable.</p>",
